reported, proposed 4 times: source positions compared column by column regardless of line: Span.contained checks startCol<=other.startCol and endCol>=other.endCol even when the lines differ (Span(1:10..5:2) does not contain Span(2:1..3:50)); Token.makeSpan floors the end column of a one line last token at first.column() even when first is on an earlier line (a at 1:10 to x at 3:1 gives 1:10..3:10)
refused, proposed 6 times: columns counted in code points by the tokenizer (MetaTokenizer, Token.makeSpan) but used as UTF-16 char indexes by Message (nextVisible/prevVisible charAt(col-1), visualCol, visualDelta, tabAwareWidth, expandTabs): after a character outside the BMP the caret shrinks the wrong span and tabs expand to the wrong stop ("\uD83D\uDE00a b" span 1:2..1:4 underlines "a " only): unreachable, every source is checked against Fs.allowed first and tokenizing stops at the first character outside it (see ruledOut)
reported, proposed 1 time: Message splits the raw source on \R while MetaTokenizer drops a leading BOM and breaks lines only at \n: in a file starting with a BOM every error on line 1 shows a ? in front and the marker one column off; the illegal character error on a form feed shows the text before it as the whole line, with no caret
refused, proposed 2 times: tab widths in Message caret math measured from the wrong start column: visualCol counts tab stops from 1 while expandTabs draws them from 0, and visualDelta measures tabs inside a span as if the span began at column 0 ("a\tx" marks the space before x; "ab\txyz" span 1:2..1:4 gets one caret too many): unreachable for the same reason
reported, proposed 1 time: truncation counting the "..." ellipsis as one character: PrettyFileName.shorten keeps maxLen-1 characters and prepends "..." in its n<=2 and last resort branches, returning 82 characters for a limit of 80 (mem:aaa..100 chars gives "..." plus 79 a); PrettyToken.show keeps 21 plus "..." for 24, correctly; made moot: file names are no longer truncated (relative to the working directory or absolute, like compilers do)
